      en: "Calauit Safari Park, located on Calauit Island in Palawan, is a unique wildlife sanctuary established in 1976. It was created through a presidential decree to provide a haven for African wildlife and to help propagate endangered local species. The park covers an area of approximately 3,700 hectares, featuring a mix of savanna-like plains, forests, and mangrove swamps, which mimics the natural habitat of the African animals. Visitors can see giraffes, zebras, elands, and gazelles roaming freely alongside native Philippine animals like the Calamian deer, Palawan peacock-pheasant, and sea turtles. The park was initially a response to an appeal by the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) to help save endangered African wildlife. Over the years, it has become a significant conservation project and an ecotourism destination, offering a distinct safari experience in Southeast Asia. The journey to the island itself is an adventure, often involving a boat trip that provides scenic views of the surrounding Calamian archipelago."
